Super excited to get funded by @schmidtsciences to study computer-use agents (CUAs) under adversarial attacks. Many thanks to the student leads including @LiaoZeyi, Jaylen Jones, Linxi Jiang, and amazing co-PIs
@ysu_nlp and @cszlin.
As the capabilities of CUAs improve, evaluating and mitigating their safety/security risks in real-world environments becomes crucial. Through this project, we aim to conduct comprehensive evaluation and effective mitigation of the adversarial risks of these agents. We plan to release some exciting work next week. Stay tuned!

clean_summary:
Labs starting to pull up the ladders on the ability to diffuse AI was inevitable. Doing it without telling the user is misaligned.

@Hangsiin: "When Fable 5 is used for frontier LLM development, it does not notify the user and instead limits the model’s capabilities through methods such as prompt modification, steering vectors, and PEFT."

Anthropic estimated that this would affect approximately 0.03% of traffic.

Guess where @ZhihanLiu21628 is revisiting these cool ideas. 

@fnruji316625: "Just revisited RAFA (Reason for Future, Act for Now) from ICML 2024. It’s honestly surprising how forward-looking this work was. Back in 2024, they were already framing LLM agents as a closed-loop learning, planning, and acting system: Reason for future, act for now; memory-driven replanning; long-horizon planning; sample-efficient interaction; even with theoretical regret guarantees. 

Looking at today’s wave of autonomous agents, online reinforcement learning, test-time adaptation, and memory-based reasoning, many of these ideas feel remarkably prescient. Definitely a paper worth revisiting if you’re working on LLM agents.
